## Performance Analysis

**(i) Data Structure:** Maintain spanning forest in RAM, buffered edges on disk with locality-aware partitioning, and periodic rebuild of compact connectivity structure.

**(ii) Amortized I/O:** O((n + mb)/B) per batch, where rebuild occurs every T/b batches.

**(iii) Query Time:** O(log_B n) average case, O(m/B) worst case when searching residual edges.

**(iv) Space:** O(n + m + T) where T is buffer threshold, typically Θ(n + m).

**(v) Optimizations:** 1) Adaptive rebuild threshold based on update/query ratio; 2) Multi-level buffering with hot/cold edge classification.

**(vi) Comparison:** Recomputing from scratch costs O((n+m)α(n,m)/B) per batch vs. O((n+mb)/B) amortized with batching—significant improvement when b << m.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical sliding-window counting and maintaining top-k with bounded memory.

## Key Concepts
- Sliding window data structure
- Hashmap for counts
- Top-k extraction in bounded time
- Deterministic tie-breaking

## Code Example
````javascript
// Sketch
const W = 10000;
const windowQ = [];
const counts = new Map();
function applyBatch(batch){
  for (const x of batch){
    windowQ.push(x);
    counts.set(x, (counts.get(x) || 0) + 1);
    if (windowQ.length > W){
      const y = windowQ.shift();
      const ny = (counts.get(y) - 1);
      if (ny) counts.set(y, ny);
      else counts.delete(y);
    }
  }
  const top3 = [...counts.entries()].sort((a,b)=>b[1]-a[1]).slice(0,3).map(e=>e[0]);
  return top3;
}
````

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt to a dynamic window size?
- What optimizations exist to avoid full re-scan on every batch?

